---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

const repo = "https://github.com/jakejarvis/Clicker";
---

<Layout
  title="Help — Clicker"
  description="Answers to common questions about Clicker, and how to report a problem."
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>Help</h1>
    <p class="muted">
      Answers to the questions that come up most. If yours isn't here, <a
        href="#report">report an issue</a
      >.
    </p>

    <h2 id="not-found">Clicker can't find my Apple TV</h2>
    <p>
      Make sure your Mac and Apple TV are on the same network, and that Clicker
      is allowed under <strong
        >System Settings › Privacy & Security › Local Network</strong
      >. Without that permission Clicker can't see anything on your network, and
      the panel says so. If the Apple TV is asleep, wake it with its own remote.
      One that was just plugged in can take a minute to show up.
    </p>

    <h2 id="pairing">Pairing doesn't work</h2>
    <p>
      On the Apple TV, open <strong
        >Settings › Remotes and Devices › Remote App and Devices</strong
      > and check that new devices are allowed to pair, then click <strong
        >Pair…</strong
      > in Clicker again. If you mistype the code, click <strong>Try Again</strong> for
      a new one.
    </p>

    <h2 id="pair-again">My Apple TV asks me to pair again</h2>
    <p>
      The Apple TV was reset, or Clicker was removed from its list of remotes.
      Either way, the old pairing no longer works. Click <strong>Pair…</strong> and
      enter the new code.
    </p>

    <h2 id="not-responding">My Apple TV stopped responding</h2>
    <p>
      Forget it in Clicker's Settings, under Paired Devices, and pair again.
    </p>

    <h2 id="volume">Volume and Mute don't do anything</h2>
    <p>
      They control your TV or receiver only if the Apple TV does, through
      HDMI-CEC or an IR remote it has learned. You can check under <strong
        >Settings › Remotes and Devices › Volume Control</strong
      > on the Apple TV. If Mute has no effect on your setup, Clicker turns the button
      off after the first try.
    </p>

    <h2 id="swipes">Swipes go the wrong way</h2>
    <p>
      Two-finger swipes over the clickpad move the TV's focus the way your fingers
      move, like the touch surface on the Siri Remote, whatever your Mac's scrolling
      setting. If you'd rather swipes move the content, set <strong
        >Swipe Direction</strong
      > to Inverted in Settings.
    </p>

    <h2 id="where">Where's the window or Dock icon?</h2>
    <p>
      There isn't one. Clicker lives in the menu bar: click its icon to open the
      remote, or right-click it for Settings, About and Quit. You can also set a
      keyboard shortcut in Settings that opens the remote from any app.
    </p>

    <h2 id="keyboard">Keyboard shortcuts</h2>
    <p>While the panel is open you can leave the mouse alone:</p>
    <table class="keys">
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Key</th><th scope="col">Does</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Arrow keys</td><td>Move</td></tr>
        <tr><td><kbd>Return</kbd></td><td>Select</td></tr>
        <tr><td><kbd>Delete</kbd></td><td>Back</td></tr>
        <tr><td><kbd>Space</kbd> or <kbd>P</kbd></td><td>Play/Pause</td></tr>
        <tr><td><kbd>H</kbd> or <kbd>T</kbd></td><td>TV (Home)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Hold <kbd>S</kbd></td><td>Siri</td></tr>
        <tr><td><kbd>M</kbd></td><td>Mute</td></tr>
        <tr><td><kbd>+</kbd> and <kbd>−</kbd></td><td>Volume up and down</td></tr>
        <tr><td><kbd>⌘,</kbd></td><td>Settings</td></tr>
        <tr><td><kbd>Esc</kbd></td><td>Close the panel</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Hold an arrow key to keep moving, as you would hold the clickpad on the Siri
      Remote. Hover over any button to see its shortcut.
    </p>

    <h2 id="updates">How do I update Clicker?</h2>
    <p>
      The version from this site or Homebrew checks for updates once a day and
      shows a notice under the remote when one is ready. You can check yourself
      with <strong>Check for Updates…</strong> in the ••• menu. The Mac App Store
      version is updated by the App Store.
    </p>

    <h2 id="report">Report an issue</h2>
    <p>
      Something broken, or a question that isn't answered here? Open an issue on
      GitHub. It helps to include your Clicker version (click it in <strong
        >About Clicker</strong
      > to copy it), your macOS version, your Apple TV model and what you expected
      to happen.
    </p>
  </article>

  <p class="help-report">
    <a class="download" href={`${repo}/issues/new`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      Report an Issue on GitHub
    </a>
  </p>
</Layout>
